Subject: Report the exception specification a C++ function was declared with

bindgen records no exception specification anywhere: `noexcept` does not appear
in its sources, `FunctionSig` has no field for one, and nothing it generates
carries the fact. Neither does any ABI mangle one into a symbol name, so a
consumer cannot recover it from a `#[link_name]` the way it can a ref-qualifier.

A consumer which emits C++ of its own needs it. C++ rejects an override whose
exception specification is looser than that of the virtual function it
overrides, so a generated override of `virtual void f() noexcept` which does not
repeat the `noexcept` is ill-formed and the generated code does not compile.

libclang has the answer and bindgen never asks:
`clang_getCursorExceptionSpecificationType` has been there since libclang 5.0
and clang-sys declares it behind a feature bindgen's `clang_11_0` request
already chains down to. Wrap it in `clang.rs`, record the kind on the function
at parse time, and report it through an additive
`ParseCallbacks::denote_exception_specification`.

The kinds are reported as libclang reports them rather than folded into "throws"
and "does not throw", because several of them say only that libclang does not
know which exceptions the function allows. `ComputedNoexcept` covers
`noexcept(false)` as well as `noexcept(true)`: clang has evaluated the operand,
and the type's spelling carries the operand's text, but nothing carries the value
it evaluated to and there is no cursor for the operand to evaluate. Under
`-std=c++17` and later the canonical function type resolves it, because the
specification is then part of the type; under C++14 the canonical type drops
every specification, so there is nothing to resolve it with. Folding the kind
here would make the answer depend on the language version without saying so. A
consumer which needs the distinction can see that it is not available, and one
which needs the types `throw(A, B)` names can see the same.
